{"count": 6, "description": "SUBWAY CREW (DRIVER) SCHEDULING — MILP, minimize number of drivers, output optimal task chains.

=== PROBLEM DATA ===
Line with 40 stations; only stations A, M, Z are "attendance / handover" stations where a driver may take over or hand over a train. All 200 driving tasks already have pickup and drop-off stations in {A, M, Z}.

200 tasks i = 1..200, each with: train number, train ID (physical train), pickup time (clock), pickup station, drop-off time, drop-off station. Full task table is given (e.g. task 331: train 331 / train ID M16, pickup 10:03:11 at M, drop-off 10:57:43 at Z; task 163: train 163 / N23, pickup 10:04:10 at A, drop-off 10:39:11 at M; task 1: L06, pickup 10:03:43 at Z, drop-off 10:58:32 at M; task 332: N27, 10:04:32 M -> 10:40:10 A; task 334: L01, 10:10:32 M -> 10:46:10 A). Task durations range ~35 to ~55 minutes. Tasks cover roughly 10:03 to 15:58.

Mileage table (km): A->M = 17.1, Z->M = 26.4. Assume the line is bidirectional so mileage(M->A)=17.1 and mileage(M->Z)=26.4; each task's mileage is the mileage of its (pickup,drop-off) pair.

=== PARAMETERS (assumptions, to be stated explicitly) ===
tau_min = 30 min : minimum interval between drop-off of task i and pickup of the next task j (changeover + walking to the next train). Exception: if task j continues the SAME physical train (same train ID) and starts exactly when task i ends (t_pu(j) = t_do(i)), the driver continues driving the same train with tau_min = 0.
T_c = 120 min : maximum CONTINUOUS driving time without a real break. A gap of at least R_min = 30 min between two consecutive tasks counts as a break that resets the continuous-driving counter.
D_max = 150 km : maximum total mileage of one driver's duty.
L_max = 480 min : maximum duty span (first task pickup time to last task drop-off time), including breaks.
Handover must occur at A, M, or Z (data already compliant).

=== REQUIRED MODEL CONTENT ===
Give a fully written MILP with explicit sets, parameters (with numeric values), decision variables (binary / continuous / integer), an objective function, and all constraints written as numbered equations with short explanations. Cover:
(1) coverage: every task is performed exactly once;
(2) chaining: each task has at most one predecessor and one successor, a chain is a path in the time-forward DAG of feasible connections (so subtour elimination is not needed, but prove/justify);
(3) number of drivers = number of chain starts = number of chain ends, minimized;
(4) attendance/handover location constraint;
(5) minimum connection time / changeover time;
(6) maximum continuous driving time (needs cumulative "continuous driving since last break" variables with correct big-M logic for both reset arcs and carry-over arcs);
(7) maximum mileage per duty (cumulative chain-mileage variables);
(8) maximum duty span (cumulative chain start-time variables);
optionally (9) fairness / even task allocation tie-breaker (e.g. lexicographic secondary objective or a max-min workload) and (10) a way to read out the optimal task chains (successor lists) and the connection table.

=== MODELING STYLE TO EXPLORE (please produce different variants so they can be compared) ===
Variant 1: arc-based / "flow" formulation with big-M auxiliary variables for cumulative continuous-driving time, cumulative mileage and chain start time, plus start/end indicators; DAG structure guarantees acyclicity.
Variant 2: set-partitioning / column-generation style: variables = complete feasible chains (paths) with cover constraints and a driver-count constraint, with a pricing subproblem (shortest path with resource constraints) and an equivalent extended (arc) formulation; describe how the LP relaxation differs.
Variant 3: time-indexed / time-discretized formulation over a 30-min or 1-min grid with on/off-duty and driving state variables per driver, or a multi-commodity flow formulation.
Variant 4: MILP with time-expanded network + MTZ-like or flow-based subtour prevention, binary y[i,j] arc variables and driver count via number of source flows.

For each variant give: exact variable definitions, full constraint system, objective, and a short discussion of strengths/weaknesses (LP relaxation tightness, model size, integrality, computational tractability for 200 tasks / ~ thousands of feasible arcs). Finally recommend the formulation that should be implemented, and for the recommended one provide the complete final equations, the values of all parameters, the feasible-arc generation rule, and the algorithm for extracting the optimal task chains (allocation and connection relationship of all 200 tasks)."}

